{
  "classifications": [
    {
      "statement": "Only one group in ATT&CK uses all 16 techniques the report describes: G0094 Kimsuky (T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information, T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel, T1053 Scheduled Task/Job (through its sub-technique T1053.005 Scheduled Task), T1055 Process Injection, T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter (through its sub-techniques T1059.001 PowerShell, T1059.003 Windows Command Shell, T1059.005 Visual Basic, T1059.006 Python and T1059.007 JavaScript), T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer, T1115 Clipboard Data, T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information, T1204 User Execution (through its sub-techniques T1204.001 Malicious Link, T1204.002 Malicious File and T1204.004 Malicious Copy and Paste), T1218 System Binary Proxy Execution (through its sub-techniques T1218.005 Mshta, T1218.010 Regsvr32 and T1218.011 Rundll32), T1497 Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion (through its sub-technique T1497.001 System Checks), T1547 Boot or Logon Autostart Execution (through its sub-technique T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder), T1555 Credentials from Password Stores (through its sub-technique T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers), T1566 Phishing, T1620 Reflective Code Loading and T1685 Disable or Modify Tools).",
      "reason": "The context provides the Lab52 report but no MITRE ATT&CK group records or group-to-technique mappings. It never mentions Kimsuky or G0094, so it cannot establish that this group uses every listed technique, much less that it is the only group to do so. The report does describe behaviors consistent with several listed techniques, including obfuscation, scheduled tasks, process injection, PowerShell execution, clipboard monitoring, registry persistence, reflective assembly loading, and disabling security protections. However, it does not explicitly enumerate 16 ATT&CK techniques or supply their identifiers. Several listed sub-techniques also lack support in the report, including Python execution, malicious copy and paste, Regsvr32, Rundll32, and virtualization system checks; its sandbox-evasion discussion instead concerns executing macros when the document closes. Thus, partial support for individual behaviors does not substantiate the claimed comprehensive and exclusive ATT&CK attribution to Kimsuky.",
